WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:15.549
Good morning John; it's Wednesday, January
17th.

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:20.850
Hi Maureen Johnson and E. Lockhart! Maureen...
is that the Maureen that told me to build

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.360
a business card house out of my misprinted
business cards? If it is, then I have a surprise

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.860
for her! Last night, I spent two hours talking
to a libertarian on the internet. He was a

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.140
friend of mine from high school and I don't
know what happened. He says there shouldn't

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.270
be regulations on nuclear power plants!

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:45.390
Last night, I also blew my nose for fifteen
straight minutes, and then at the end of it,

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:53.800
I had converted six ounces of toilet paper
into two pounds of mushy goo, but now I can

00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:04.170
do this! (breathes in through nose deeply)
Ahhh.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.490
I'm just going to jump right into Misprinted
Business Card Utilization ideas. Maureen from

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:12.030
the comments suggested that I build a business
card house. I didn't know how to build card

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.740
houses, so I went on the internet and did
some research. There's the basic card house,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:22.280
where you use, you know, this and this and
then you stack until it gets high up, but

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.710
that's not the best way to build card houses
it turns out. I found out the way that the

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:32.639
professional card stackers do it. And now,
I'm going to teach you and all of our viewers

00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:37.310
how to stack cards, the awesome way!

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:45.079
We create a T. And we lean this card on this
card, thus stabilizing this card and this

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:46.249
card.

00:01:46.249 --> 00:01:48.450
Is that guy wearing jammie pants?

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.490
And this guy on this guy.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:56.179
Yes, that guy is wearing jammie pants! It's
early and I'm sick and tired, okay?

00:01:56.179 --> 00:02:06.899
And you lean this guy on that guy, then you
lean this guy on that guy. Here we've got

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:12.990
the first story of a really good card house.
And now we add the roof; this is important

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:24.560
because we're going to be stacking more stories
on it. Oh. And... Voilà! And there we have

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.840
the first story of a card house.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:44.349
(unintelligible squeaky sped up voice)

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:50.239
Let us take a look at this thing of beauty.
An excellent Misprinted Business Card Utilization

00:02:50.239 --> 00:02:56.709
idea; especially because now I know how to
stack cards! And hopefully, so do you.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.349
So, I can't just let this go; I'm going to
have to try and add another story.

00:02:59.349 --> 00:03:04.620
(squeaky voice chatters, exclaims)

